const rows = [];
function add(id, history, ...keys) {
  rows.push({ wopId: `wop-${String(id).padStart(6, '0')}`, history,
    sources: keys.map(key => ({ ...refs[key], sourceId: `${refs[key].sourceId}-${String(id).padStart(6, '0')}` })) });
}
add(1, 'Polyethylene became an industrial material after Eric Fawcett and Reginald Gibson obtained it during high-pressure experiments at ICI in 1933. The earliest commercial route produced branched material now called LDPE. A second major development came in the early 1950s, when Phillips researchers J. Paul Hogan and Robert Banks developed chromium-catalyzed polyethylene. These discoveries opened distinct branches of the family rather than a single interchangeable resin. Subsequent gas-phase production and comonomer control broadened the range: Union Carbide brought its UNIPOL route to LLDPE in 1977. Packaging, containers and pipe became enduring markets as catalyst and reactor development allowed producers to tailor the material.', 'ici-pe', 'phillips-polyolefins', 'unipol');
add(2, 'LDPE is the branched polyethylene associated with ICI’s 1933 discovery by Eric Fawcett and Reginald Gibson, rather than a later invention of an independent polymer backbone. Its early history was strongly shaped by electrical insulation: polyethylene insulated wartime radar cables. After the war, manufacturers expanded into consumer packaging. The British Plastics Federation identifies Monsanto’s 1945 squeeze bottle and the 1949 American launch of polyethylene Tupperware as visible milestones in this transition. Flexible containers and packaging helped turn a specialist insulating material into a familiar household plastic.', 'ici-pe', 'bpf-timeline');
add(3, 'LLDPE emerged from efforts to give low-density ethylene copolymers a controlled, predominantly linear backbone. Univation records the 1977 startup of Union Carbide’s first UNIPOL LLDPE plant in Texas as a major commercialization milestone. Gas-phase production helped establish the class, and later metallocene catalysts expanded its performance range. Packaging and merchandise bags, agricultural films, crates and household containers became important outlets; metallocene grades also developed stretch-film and multilayer-packaging markets. These milestones connect control of comonomer incorporation with production technology and film fabrication, rather than assigning one invention date to every LLDPE grade.', 'unipol', 'univation-uses');
add(4, 'MDPE is a density-defined part of the polyethylene family, so assigning it a single molecular invention date would be misleading. A documented industrial milestone is DuPont’s manufacture of Aldyl-A MDPE pipe for gas distribution beginning in the 1960s. Gas utilities became a major historical market. The product line passed to Uponor in 1991–1992, illustrating how material development continued through changes in producer. The Gas Technology Institute’s historical assessment also records changes in pipe-grade designations and investigations of slow crack growth. Those service and formulation histories belong to particular generations of pipe; they do not establish one performance history for every MDPE grade.', 'mdpe-gti');
add(5, 'HDPE’s early commercial history includes the chromium-catalyst work of J. Paul Hogan and Robert Banks at Phillips in 1951–1952. Phillips introduced Marlex polyethylene in 1954. Initial sales were uneven, but the hula-hoop boom absorbed production while the company developed additional grades; pipe, bottles and other containers followed. Karl Ziegler’s separate 1953 catalyst work provided another important route to linear polyethylene. These parallel advances explain why HDPE has multiple industrial lineages. Its later uses expanded across gas and water distribution, chemical containers and automotive parts as producers learned to control molecular structure and processing.', 'phillips-polyolefins', 'lyb-history');
add(6, 'UHMWPE developed within the low-pressure polyethylene revolution. Celanese traces its GUR lineage to Karl Ziegler’s 1953 catalyst work, an Oberhausen pilot plant and public introduction at the 1955 K trade fair. Early wear applications included textile-machine shuttles replacing wood; later markets included industrial wear surfaces, orthopedic bearings and porous separators. A separate processing advance made extremely strong fibers possible: DSM patented gel spinning in the 1970s and commercialized Dyneema in the 1980s. Rope, fishing nets, cut-resistant gloves and protective armor became major outlets for this oriented-fiber form. Fiber manufacture and molded UHMWPE therefore have related but distinct histories.', 'gur', 'dyneema');
add(7, 'VLDPE developed as a more flexible extension of linear ethylene copolymers rather than as a wholly new backbone. A contemporary packaging patent records Union Carbide VLDPE grades described in 1984, including one aimed at film and another emphasizing toughness and clarity. W. R. Grace’s subsequent work on shrinkable packaging films documents how converters turned these resins into useful products. The important historical direction was to obtain flexibility through copolymer structure and to tune molecular-weight distribution for extrusion. These dated records establish commercial development in the mid-1980s, without requiring a single disputed launch year for the entire VLDPE class.', 'vldpe-film');
add(8, 'Crosslinking extended polyethylene into hot-water pipe and other applications where ordinary thermoplastic behavior was limiting. For pipe, Thomas Engel’s work in the 1960s was a central milestone; European manufacturers developed commercial peroxide-crosslinked tubing from that approach. The Plastics Pipe Institute traces the subsequent North American adoption through radiant heating, snow melting and eventually broader plumbing use. Wirsbo, later Uponor, and other manufacturers contributed material, joining-system and market development. PEX history also includes distinct silane and radiation routes, so the Engel pipe lineage should not be treated as the invention of every crosslinked polyethylene product.', 'pex');
add(9, 'Polypropylene arose from several parallel catalyst programs in the early 1950s. Phillips researchers J. Paul Hogan and Robert Banks observed crystalline polypropylene in 1951; Karl Ziegler’s and Giulio Natta’s work established other foundational catalyst and stereochemical advances. Patent priority was contested for decades. Commercial development subsequently made polypropylene important in packaging, household goods, fibers and automotive parts. Its history continued through reactor and catalyst design: LyondellBasell records the introduction of Spherizone in 2002 as a route to additional molecular architectures. The family consequently includes materials developed through distinct stereochemical and copolymer strategies.', 'phillips-polyolefins', 'lyb-history');
add(10, 'The isotactic branch of polypropylene is associated especially with Giulio Natta’s 1954 stereospecific-polymerization work and the industrial lineage that became part of LyondellBasell. The ability to organize propylene units regularly made crystalline polypropylene a practical material. Later developments concentrated on selective catalysts and more efficient polymerization, broadening applications in molded goods, packaging and fibers. Modern Spheripol technology illustrates this continuity: successive catalyst generations reduced processing complexity while extending the homopolymer and copolymer product range. These are developments of isotactic manufacture, not new dates of invention for the original chain structure.', 'lyb-history', 'spheripol');
add(11, 'Syndiotactic polypropylene had a different development path from the dominant isotactic material. Early syndiospecific catalyst work established that alternating stereochemistry was possible, but later metallocene chemistry made much higher stereoregularity accessible. John Ewen and Abbas Razavi’s Fina patent lineage, with a 1988 priority date, documents a major step in that development. Their work linked catalyst symmetry and polymer microstructure to a distinct crystalline polypropylene. The patent discusses uses in shaped articles, fibers and films. It records a technical milestone and intended applications; it does not establish that syndiotactic grades displaced isotactic polypropylene on a comparable commercial scale.', 'spp');
add(12, 'Random propylene–ethylene copolymers grew out of the post-1950s polypropylene industry as manufacturers used comonomers to adjust an otherwise crystalline homopolymer. Their history is a sequence of catalyst and reactor improvements rather than a single independently documented invention. LyondellBasell’s Spheripol documentation describes successive high-selectivity catalysts and bulk/gas-phase process refinements that support commercial random-copolymer manufacture. Alongside broader polypropylene developments such as Spherizone in 2002, these tools widened the range of packaging and molded products that manufacturers could design. A particular process launch dates an industrial capability, not the first synthesis of all random propylene copolymers.', 'spheripol', 'lyb-history');
add(13, 'Heterophasic polypropylene developed as producers combined a polypropylene matrix with an elastomeric phase to broaden the material’s impact performance. It is a reactor-made multiphase material class, with no single first-invention date established by the sources used here. LyondellBasell documents the role of gas-phase copolymerization stages and successive Spheripol catalyst generations in expanding impact-copolymer production. Such process development supported the polypropylene industry’s expansion in automotive parts, appliances and other molded goods. The 2002 Spherizone milestone provides a later example of producers changing reactor architecture to gain additional control over product architecture.', 'spheripol', 'lyb-history');
add(14, 'Poly(1-butene) belongs to the first wave of stereospecific polyolefins. LyondellBasell traces its discovery to Giulio Natta’s research group in 1954 and industrial introduction in Europe to the mid-1960s. Flexible pressure piping became an important early application, with subsequent development directed toward hot- and cold-water systems. The company’s later PB-1 business also developed packaging, polymer-modification and adhesive uses. This history connects an early catalyst discovery to decades of pipe-grade development; the performance of a particular pipe system still depends on its resin generation, fittings and installation.', 'pb1');
add(15, 'ICI introduced poly(4-methyl-1-pentene) as TPX in 1965, according to a contemporary UNIDO paper. Mitsui Chemicals subsequently became a major developer and producer of the material. Its commercial development has emphasized an unusual combination of transparency, heat resistance and release behavior. Established uses include laboratory vessels, electrical insulation, rubber-hose mandrels and release films. More specialized applications include hollow fibers and processing aids for electronics. These applications explain its place as a specialty polyolefin rather than a direct replacement for commodity polyethylene or polypropylene.', 'unido-tpx', 'tpx');
add(16, 'The modern commercial COP history is illustrated by Zeon’s hydrogenated cyclic-polymer materials. Zeon completed its ZEONEX plant at Mizushima in November 1990 and began selling ZEONOR in September 1998. Optical applications were particularly important because dimensional stability and low moisture uptake complemented transparency; medical and industrial molded parts broadened the market. Zeon’s history therefore records a transition from cyclic-monomer chemistry to precision-use plastics. These milestones date specific commercial families, while COP as a broader chemical category can include materials with different research and production lineages.', 'zeon-history', 'zeon-cop');
add(17, 'Cyclic olefin copolymers became commercially important when catalyst advances enabled controlled incorporation of cyclic and linear olefins. TOPAS traces its lineage to Hoechst research in the early 1990s and a commercial plant at Oberhausen in 2000. The business later passed through Ticona/Celanese to Daicel and Polyplastics in 2006. Early development targeted transparent, dimensionally stable products, including optical data storage and lenses; pharmaceutical packaging, diagnostic disposables and barrier films became important markets. These dates describe the TOPAS industrial lineage rather than the first laboratory preparation of every cyclic olefin copolymer.', 'topas', 'topas-brochure');
add(18, 'EVA developed as an ethylene copolymer offering flexibility and adhesion useful in many formulations. DuPont’s ELVAX was imported by the Mitsui Japanese venture from 1962, followed by domestic EVAFLEX production in 1965. This is an international commercialization milestone rather than EVA’s first synthesis. The subsequent product range served footwear and packaging foams, hot-melt adhesives, agricultural and protective films, electrical-wire coverings and molded goods. Development of different vinyl-acetate contents and processing grades allowed these markets to diverge while retaining the same basic ethylene–vinyl acetate chemistry.', 'mdp-history', 'evaflex-uses');
add(19, 'Kuraray began the research program that led to EVOH in 1957 while seeking useful plastics derived from its vinyl-acetate and PVA chemistry. Discovery of the copolymer’s gas-barrier behavior was followed by fifteen years of development before EVAL commercialization in 1972. Coextrusion was a major enabling advance because EVOH could be combined with other plastics in multilayer packaging. Food films and condiment containers were prominent early markets. Hollow-fiber artificial-kidney membranes and, from the 1990s, automotive fuel tanks extended its use. This is a company-documented commercialization history, not a claim that a single grade serves all those applications.', 'eval');
add(20, 'Ethylene–acrylic acid copolymers developed as functional polyethylenes whose acid groups improved interaction with other materials. A documented milestone is the Dow patent by John Thomson and George Waples, published in 1970, describing their preparation and coating behavior. It shows that research was already focused on balancing polyethylene-like processability with adhesion, rather than merely establishing that the monomers could react. Coatings and laminated structures became an important direction for the class. This patent provides a dated industrial contribution; it should not be read as proof of the first EAA synthesis or of a universal commercial formulation.', 'eaa-patent');
add(21, 'EMAA developed within the acid-functional ethylene-copolymer industry. By 1988, a General Electric patent described DuPont NUCREL as an established commercial source and investigated its use in engineering-plastic blends. Dow-Mitsui’s corporate chronology records full Japanese NUCREL production in 1985. Coatings, films, adhesive layers, wire applications and molded articles became outlets for the unneutralized acid copolymer. Its history overlaps with ionomers, but neutralizing acid groups creates a separate material development. These records establish commercial availability and subsequent applications without assigning an unsupported first-synthesis date to the entire EMAA class.', 'nucrel-1988', 'mdp-history', 'nucrel');
add(22, 'Polyolefin ionomers emerged commercially through partially neutralized acid-containing ethylene copolymers. DuPont’s SURLYN reached Japanese imports through the Mitsui venture in 1964. Domestic manufacture began in autumn 1978 under the HIMILAN name, followed by expanded production. Golf-ball covers became a recognizable application, while films and heat-sealing layers supported meat, snack and beverage packaging. Cosmetic-container components and tool grips provided other molded uses. This lineage traces a commercial ionic-polymer platform, whose compositions and neutralizing ions vary; it does not assign one invention date to every ion-containing polyolefin.', 'mdp-history', 'himilan-uses');
add(23, 'PVC’s origins precede its useful commercial formulations. Henri Victor Regnault studied vinyl chloride in 1835, and Eugen Baumann reported its light-induced conversion to a white solid in 1872. Fritz Klatte’s early-twentieth-century work at Griesheim-Elektron advanced monomer and polymerization chemistry. At BFGoodrich in 1926, Waldo Semon found that combining the rigid polymer with suitable liquids produced flexible material, opening a practical path to plasticized PVC. Coated fabrics, electrical insulation and other vinyl goods followed. The distinction is between early polymer observation and later formulation and manufacturing advances; rigid and plasticized PVC products subsequently developed different applications.', 'semon', 'michel-pvc');
add(24, 'CPVC developed as a way to extend the service range of PVC, particularly in piping. Lubrizol traces its BFGoodrich predecessor’s CPVC development to 1959 and the subsequent commercialization of FlowGuard plumbing systems. Pipe and fittings for hot- and cold-water supply became the defining early market. Continued work involved compound formulation, joining methods and the practical manufacture of complete systems. A later expansion milestone was the 2020 partnership announced with Grasim for resin production in India. These dates describe one major industrial lineage; the original resin, later branded compounds and installed piping systems are separate historical subjects.', 'cpvc');
add(25, 'PVDC’s industrial history is closely associated with Ralph Wiley’s 1933 discovery at Dow Chemical and the subsequent Saran resin business. Early products included insect screens, chemical-plant pipes and fishing line; the Powerhouse museum holds Saran fishing equipment made in 1944. Dow’s national introduction of household Saran Wrap in 1953 made barrier packaging a familiar consumer application. The history therefore extends from industrial and wartime products to food storage and protective packaging. Saran is a trade name whose formulations can change, so a later branded film should not be identified as PVDC solely from its name.', 'dow-history', 'powerhouse-saran');
add(26, 'Poly(vinyl acetate) became an industrial resin during the early expansion of vinyl chemistry. Wacker traces its PVAc research lineage to Willy O. Herrmann’s work in 1924 and its first Burghausen production plant to 1930. The VINNAPAS product lineage subsequently developed into adhesives and binders, including materials for construction. This history reflects a change from simply making a vinyl resin to controlling how it could be delivered and used in formulations. Dispersion and powder technologies helped manufacturers serve very different markets. Wacker’s milestone dates one major production lineage, rather than establishing the first laboratory preparation of PVAc by any researcher.', 'wacker');
add(27, 'PVA’s early history reaches back to 1924, followed by extensive development in Japan. Ichiro Sakurada’s research group produced a Japanese synthetic-fiber precursor in 1939, and Kuraray commercialized KURALON PVA-based fiber in 1950. Producing resin for those fibers created the technical foundation for a broader PVA business. Kuraray began merchant PVA resin production in 1958, initially targeting textile sizing and paper treatment; resin sales eventually outgrew the company’s fiber consumption. The story therefore joins polymer chemistry, fiber processing and later water-soluble binder applications, with distinct dates for discovery and industrial commercialization.', 'pva');
add(28, 'PVB became important through the search for better interlayers in laminated safety glass. A National Park Service account of aircraft-cockpit materials identifies a 1939 development effort involving DuPont, Libbey-Owens-Ford, Pittsburgh Plate Glass, Monsanto and Carbide and Carbon Chemical Corporation. The new interlayer addressed limitations of earlier cellulose-based laminates and became an enduring safety-glass material. Automotive and aircraft glazing gave PVB a practical role in holding fractured glass together. This milestone concerns safety-glass development and adoption; it is not a claim that every participating company independently invented the polymer in that year.', 'cockpit-plastics');
add(29, 'Poly(vinyl formal) was already a practical electrical-insulation material during the Second World War. The Powerhouse museum preserves a 1943 specimen made by Australian General Electric and describes its display as an example of a material that could replace rubber in wire covering. That surviving industrial object provides a firm date and named manufacturer for an early application. It also shows how wartime material shortages encouraged the use of vinyl-derived plastics. The collection does not establish the first synthesis or complete international commercialization history of poly(vinyl formal), so 1943 is an application milestone rather than an invention date.', 'pvformal');
add(30, 'Polystyrene’s history begins with Eduard Simon’s 1839 observation of styrene forming a solid material. BASF developed industrial production in 1930, following styrene work at Ludwigshafen in 1929; Dow introduced Styron in 1937. It grew into solid molded products and expanded materials for packaging, household articles and insulation. The EPA’s 1980 account records an established producer base including Dow, Monsanto and BASF. Copolymers and rubber-modified products became neighboring branches of styrenic materials. The BASF milestone identifies industrial development rather than the earliest observation of styrene forming a solid polymer.', 'unido-ps1970', 'basf-1929', 'styrenics-epa');
add(31, 'The British Plastics Federation dates commercial high-impact polystyrene to 1949. Rubber modification addressed the brittleness of ordinary polystyrene and expanded molded and sheet applications. By the EPA’s 1980 industry survey, packaging and consumer products were established uses. HIPS is consequently a postwar development branch of polystyrene whose rubber phase and manufacturing details matter, rather than a separate monomer invention.', 'bpf-timeline', 'styrenics-epa');
add(32, 'SAN was an established commercial copolymer by the EPA’s 1980 survey, which lists Dow Tyril, Monsanto Lustran SAN and ARCO/Polymers Luran. Its later development continued in transparent household components. A specific 2022 milestone is Bear Electric’s selection of INEOS Styrolution Luran SAN C for food processors, yogurt machines and filter kettles. Related grades serve drinkware, blenders and coffee-machine water containers. These sources trace industrial suppliers and sustained application development; they do not establish a unique first laboratory synthesis of SAN.', 'styrenics-epa', 'ineos-san2022');
add(33, 'The British Plastics Federation records ABS production in 1948, during the postwar expansion of styrenic plastics. Rubber toughening of a rigid styrene–acrylonitrile phase supported drainpipes, appliances, automotive parts and luggage. By 1980, the EPA listed Borg-Warner Cycolac, Monsanto Lustran, Dow, BFGoodrich and Uniroyal among producers. These commercial histories concern evolving rubber-modified material architectures; they should not be read as one immutable three-monomer formulation.', 'bpf-timeline', 'styrenics-epa');
add(34, 'BASF introduced ASA as Luran S in 1967 to offer an ABS-like material with improved weathering behavior. Replacing the vulnerable rubber chemistry with an acrylic elastomer was central to that development. The material grew into outdoor automotive trim, construction profiles, housings and other products requiring a durable appearance. INEOS’s 2023 account identifies that BASF lineage within its later Styrolution business. The history is thus tied to a specific technical need—outdoor durability—and subsequent application engineering, rather than simply the synthesis of another styrene copolymer.', 'ineos-2023');
add(35, 'MBS developed as an impact-modification technology for plastics. Kaneka records the development and marketing of KANEACE B in 1964, followed in 1973 by a Japanese polymer-science award for its manufacturing technology and completion of a Belgian plant. Later production in Malaysia extended that industrial network. PVC films, sheets, bottles, pipes and fittings became applications for MBS grades balancing toughness and transparency. These milestones trace international manufacture of a modifier used within other plastics; they do not describe MBS replacing all of those host resins as a stand-alone bulk material.', 'kaneka-history', 'kaneka-mbs-uses');
add(36, 'Styrene–maleic anhydride copolymer chemistry was documented in Arthur Voss and Ewald Dickhauser’s IG Farben patent, with German priority in 1930 and a U.S. grant in 1936. The patent already connected the copolymers to coatings, varnishes, impregnation and shaped articles. Its importance was the combination of a styrenic chain with reactive anhydride functionality, which allowed further formulation and chemical modification. This early record establishes a named research and industrial lineage, while later molding resins and functional additives represent developments of that platform rather than one unchanged commercial material.', 'sma-original');
add(37, 'SMMA developed as a transparent styrenic alternative for articles where optical appearance and molding behavior both mattered. The NAS commercial lineage, documented in INEOS Styrolution’s portfolio, serves water-filter housings, food boxes, displays, writing instruments and medical-device components. INEOS’s 2023 account also distinguishes unmodified SMMA from rubber-containing blends, an important boundary in understanding its development. The sources used here document an established industrial material and its application directions, but do not establish a defensible first-synthesis or first-commercialization date. A date attached to NAS ownership or a particular grade should not be substituted for the invention of the whole copolymer class.', 'nas', 'ineos-2023');
add(38, 'Otto Röhm’s long-running acrylic research led to transparent PMMA sheet marketed as PLEXIGLAS in 1933. Röhm’s company then extended the material from cast sheet into molding compositions and dental, automotive and other applications. Aircraft glazing was an important historical use; the National Park Service’s cockpit-materials account places acrylic glass within the transition to enclosed, lightweight aircraft structures. After wartime adoption, transparency and formability supported broader consumer and architectural uses. The brand history is one major industrial lineage of PMMA, while the polymer class includes other producers and independently developed manufacturing practices.', 'roehm', 'cockpit-plastics');
add(39, 'PAN first became commercially important through synthetic-fiber development; the British Plastics Federation records DuPont’s introduction of PAN in 1940. A later, transformative use was as a precursor to carbon fiber. In 1961, Akio Shindo at Japan’s Government Industrial Research Institute in Osaka demonstrated strong PAN-derived carbon fibers, followed by Japanese pilot production in 1964. William Watt’s work at Britain’s Royal Aircraft Establishment provided another major 1964 advance. These developments shifted PAN’s role from a textile polymer to a feedstock for structural carbon materials. Carbonization changes the material chemically, so carbon-fiber history should not be mistaken for the mechanical history of unconverted PAN.', 'bpf-timeline', 'carbon-fibers');
add(40, 'The poly(alkyl acrylate) family grew from early acrylic research, including Otto Röhm’s 1901 thesis, into major coating and adhesive technologies. After the Second World War, Harry Neher’s group at Rohm and Haas improved monomer manufacture using work derived from Walter Reppe. Benjamin Kine and Gerald Brown promoted waterborne acrylic emulsions, and the company launched Rhoplex AC-33 paint binder in 1953. Leather coatings and textile finishes preceded the large house-paint market; later products served industrial coatings and adhesives. This is a family history involving many ester monomers and formulations, not a single invention date shared by every alkyl-acrylate homopolymer.', 'acrylic-emulsions');
add(41, 'Polyacrylamide was already commercially available when Edwin R. Kolodny filed his American Cyanamid patent in 1958, granted in 1961. His work developed high-molecular-weight materials to control the viscosity of water injected for petroleum recovery, linking polymerization research to a specific industrial problem. Later producers expanded water-soluble-polymer manufacture and applications: SNF, founded in 1978, developed polyacrylamide-based products for water treatment and industrial processing. These milestones document an established polymer’s manufacturing and application history. They do not identify its first laboratory synthesis, and charged derivatives and copolymers should retain their particular composition scope.', 'pam-1961', 'snf-history');
add(42, 'Poly(acrylic acid) has a broad history that includes soluble polymers and crosslinked gels, which should not be assigned one common commercialization date. A particularly influential branch is documented by Harold Brown’s BFGoodrich patent filed in 1952 and granted in 1957. It describes lightly crosslinked acrylic-acid polymers that swell in water and function as thickeners, suspension aids and film-forming materials. This research helped establish the formulation-oriented history of acrylic-acid polymers. The milestone applies to those crosslinked compositions; it is not the first synthesis date of linear PAA or evidence that every PAA product has the same gel behavior.', 'paa-1957');

