add(43, 'Thermoplastic polyesters developed through several distinct inventions. John Rex Whinfield and James Tennant Dickson’s terephthalate-polyester work at the Calico Printers Association, with a 1941 British priority, established a route to useful synthetic fibers. The family subsequently expanded from fiber-forming PET to molding materials. Celanese dates commercial PBT to 1970, when Celanex helped establish a separate engineering-plastics branch. These milestones show how changing the glycol component and developing processing methods created different commercial materials. Fibers, films, packaging and molded parts consequently have related but different histories; there is no single introduction year for the whole family.', 'pet-original', 'pbt-celanese');
add(44, 'PET emerged from the Calico Printers Association research of John Rex Whinfield and James Tennant Dickson. Their terephthalate-polyester patent has a 1941 British priority and describes polymers that could be drawn into fibers, establishing the textile-oriented beginnings of PET. Packaging later created a second major historical role. At DuPont, Nathaniel Wyeth began investigating beverage containers in 1967 and developed a biaxially oriented PET bottle, patented in 1973, that could withstand carbonated drinks. His work connected polymer processing with container design and helped make PET bottles a familiar alternative to glass. Bottle manufacture was therefore a later application breakthrough, not the invention of PET itself.', 'pet-original', 'wyeth');
add(45, 'PETG belongs to Eastman’s development of glycol-modified terephthalate copolyesters. Charles Kibler and colleagues disclosed CHDM-based polyester chemistry in a patent filed in 1955 and issued in 1959. Intermediate glycol compositions crystallized slowly, creating a route to clear amorphous plastics. Commercial adoption followed much later: Eastman introduced Kodar PETG 6763 in 1977, initially using approximately 70% ethylene glycol and 30% CHDM in the glycol component. These copolyesters developed into a sizable plastics business because transparent, tough articles could be shaped without rapid crystallization. PET’s earlier invention date should not be assigned to PETG commercialization.', 'pct-original', 'chdm-turner');
add(46, 'PBT’s industrial history is closely associated with Celanese, whose technical manual dates its commercial introduction to 1970. Celanex established PBT as an injection-molding polyester and helped extend polyester use beyond the earlier dominance of fibers and films. Reinforced and modified grades subsequently served electrical and electronic equipment, automotive components and other engineering parts. The important commercial advance was the development of practical molding materials and processing knowledge, rather than simply substituting a glycol in a laboratory reaction. The 1970 milestone identifies commercial PBT; it does not establish the first synthesis of every butylene-terephthalate polyester.', 'pbt-celanese');
add(47, 'PTT was synthesized in 1941, but its commercial development lagged behind PET because 1,3-propanediol was expensive. Shell investigated production in the late 1960s, then introduced Corterra in 1995 after improvements in the monomer process. Carpets, apparel and industrial fibers became principal target markets. DuPont pursued a different supply route with Genencor, developing fermentation-based propanediol and marketing PTT as Sorona. DuPont’s partnership with Tate & Lyle brought bio-based monomer production to industrial scale in the 2000s. This history separates the early polymer discovery from the later feedstock and manufacturing advances that made large-scale use feasible.', 'ptt-probip');
add(48, 'Teijin traces its PEN research to 1964. Wider commercial development depended on making the naphthalene-based feedstock available economically; the company identifies Amoco’s 1995 commercialization of naphthalenedicarboxylate feedstock as an important expansion point. Teijin marketed PEN resin as Teonex for industrial components and food and pharmaceutical packaging. These uses extended the aromatic-polyester family into applications seeking additional thermal and barrier performance. The documented dates describe Teijin’s research program and a monomer-supply milestone, rather than establishing that PEN had one universally agreed invention or commercialization date.', 'pen-teijin');
add(49, 'PCT arose from Charles Kibler and colleagues’ work at Tennessee Eastman on 1,4-cyclohexanedimethanol polyesters. Their patent was filed in 1955 and issued in 1959. The crystalline terephthalate became Kodel fiber, giving PCT an early textile role. That fiber business was discontinued in the 1980s, while glass-reinforced PCT continued as a molding material for higher-temperature engineering parts. Eastman later sold that engineering business to DuPont. The sequence illustrates a polymer moving between fiber and molded-component markets; PCT should be distinguished from the later amorphous PETG and PCTG copolyesters.', 'pct-original', 'chdm-turner');
add(50, 'Polyarylates became a commercial engineering-plastics class through efforts to make aromatic polyesters useful as transparent, heat-resistant molded materials. Unitika’s corporate timeline places its U-Polymer polyarylate development in the 1970s, within the company’s diversification beyond textile fibers. Unitika subsequently developed applications in precision equipment, automotive and machinery parts, medical products, food-related goods and other manufactured articles. This provides a documented industrial lineage and period of introduction. The broader name covers multiple aromatic polyester compositions, so that product history should not be read as the first synthesis date of every polyarylate.', 'unitika-report', 'unitika-par');
add(51, 'Thermotropic aromatic copolyesters developed as researchers sought rigid polymer chains that could still be processed from a melt. An early industrial contribution is the Eastman Kodak patent filed in 1972 by William Jackson and Hans Kuhfuss and issued in 1973, describing aromatic copolyesters suitable for shaped articles and fibers. Later commercial families such as Celanese’s Vectra developed this approach into precision injection-molding materials. Electrical connectors, small electronic parts and other thin, detailed components became characteristic markets. The class encompasses different monomer combinations, so the Eastman work is a milestone in its development rather than a universal invention date for all liquid-crystalline copolyesters.', 'lcp-jackson', 'vectra-guide');
add(52, 'Synthetic polyamides grew from fundamental polymer research into a large fiber and engineering-materials family. Wallace Carothers’ DuPont team developed nylon 66 in 1935, followed by commercial fiber production and stockings. Paul Schlack’s 1938 caprolactam work provided the chemically distinct nylon 6 route associated with Perlon. Wartime demand accelerated fiber use, and subsequent applications broadened into molded and mechanical parts. These two early lineages are central to nylon history, but the family also includes later long-chain, semiaromatic and aromatic polyamides. Their discovery and introduction dates must be treated separately from the first commercial nylons.', 'carothers', 'schlack');
add(53, 'Paul Schlack developed the caprolactam route to nylon 6 in 1938, creating the material known as Perlon. This was a distinct development from DuPont’s nylon 66 condensation chemistry. Fiber production became the early industrial focus, with nylon materials becoming associated with hosiery and other textiles as well as wartime demand. Polyamide applications subsequently expanded into engineering components. The Deutsches Kunststoff Museum treats Schlack’s work as the key nylon 6 milestone within this broader transition. The name Perlon identifies an important historical commercial lineage, while PA6 is the polymer class and includes products made by many later manufacturers.', 'schlack');
add(54, 'On February 28, 1935, Wallace Carothers’ DuPont research team prepared the nylon 66 that became the basis of the company’s synthetic-fiber business. Julian Hill’s earlier work on drawing polymer fibers had helped establish the research direction. Nylon first appeared in toothbrush bristles in 1938; DuPont started commercial production at Seaford in December 1939, and nationwide stocking sales followed in May 1940. Wartime production then supplied parachutes, cords and other military textiles. This sequence distinguishes laboratory discovery, plant production and major consumer adoption. Nylon 66 subsequently became the prototype for a much broader polyamide industry.', 'carothers');

