add(55, 'PA46 became an engineering material through advances that made a high-melting aliphatic polyamide practical to manufacture. Reinoud Gaymans and Edmond Bour’s Stamicarbon patent, with 1980 priority and a 1983 grant, documents controlled prepolymer preparation followed by further condensation. DSM developed the commercial Stanyl business around heat-resistant molded components. The company subsequently broadened its markets: Stanylenka industrial yarn was commercially available from Polyamide High Performance in 1996, and DSM’s 2006 account describes airbag sewing thread, reinforcement and film applications. These are manufacturing and application milestones; the patent date is not presented as the first synthesis of PA46.', 'pa46-stamicarbon', 'stanyl-fiber');
add(56, 'PA610 was already explicitly included in Wallace Carothers’ DuPont patent on linear polyamides, filed in 1935 and granted in 1938. The patent names polyhexamethylene sebacamide and places it within the search for fiber-forming condensation polymers. Later industrial research developed its role in shaped articles and tubing. Rolando Pagilagan’s DuPont patent, filed in 1975 and issued in 1978, investigated 610-based copolyamides to address zinc-chloride exposure in flexible tubing. That later work is evidence of application-driven development from PA610 chemistry; the resulting terpolymers should not be confused with the PA610 homopolymer.', 'carothers-610', 'pagilagan');
add(57, 'PA612 was an established commercial nylon by the 1970s. A dated application milestone is the U.S. Food and Drug Administration’s July 1975 action on a DuPont petition covering nylon 612 in repeated-use food-contact articles. DuPont also pursued flexible-tubing applications: Rolando Pagilagan’s 1978 patent describes modified 612-based copolyamides intended to improve resistance to zinc chloride. Together these records show the material’s role in manufactured articles and chemically demanding service, and the importance of formulation research after commercialization. Neither record establishes its first laboratory synthesis; the 1975 decision is historical evidence rather than a statement of present regulatory suitability.', 'pa612-register', 'pagilagan');
add(58, 'The PA11 commercial lineage began with Organico’s 1947 patent and the 1949 market launch of Rilsan. Production at Serquigny established a castor-oil-derived engineering polymer whose early applications included fuel lines in the Citroën DS in the 1950s. Later expansion included a U.S. plant at Birdsboro in 1970 and growth in automotive and oil-and-gas applications during the 1980s and 1990s. Arkema’s retrospective also traces subsequent use in sports equipment, composites and additive-manufacturing powders. The history is therefore both a long-running renewable-feedstock story and a progression from tubing to increasingly specialized products.', 'rilsan70');
add(59, 'Chemische Werke Hüls presented pilot-scale VESTAMID PA12 at the K’63 trade fair in 1963 and began production at Marl in 1964. A larger reactor followed in 1966, helping supply automotive plastic components. This commercial lineage later became part of Evonik. The company’s retrospective traces PA12 from these early engineering uses into a wider portfolio, while recent work has addressed alternative feedstocks and the environmental burden of production. Those newer production options preserve the PA12 chemical identity. The Hüls dates identify a documented pilot-to-production sequence rather than proof that no earlier laboratory PA12 existed.', 'vestamid-history');
add(60, 'PA1010 has a distinct long-chain-polyamide history and should not inherit nylon 66’s invention date. A documented later development is Evonik’s March 2010 introduction of VESTAMID Terra DS compounds for high-performance cable jackets. The announcement identifies PA1010 made from castor-oil-derived monomers and targets automotive cables, buried cables and polymer optical fibers. This shows how renewable monomer supply and compound development expanded an established polymer chemistry into specialized electrical applications. The 2010 date describes that product expansion, not the first synthesis of PA1010; the available historical evidence here does not establish a single original inventor.', 'pa1010-cables');
add(61, 'PA1012 was already among the long-chain polyamides considered for automotive multilayer tubing in Degussa research with 2000 priority, later granted as a 2004 patent to Hans Ries, Guido Schmitz and Harald Haeger. The work addressed adhesion between chemically different polyamide layers while retaining the outer layer’s resistance to service conditions. A later Evonik patent application published in 2011 included PA1012 powders for layerwise molding, documenting expansion toward additive manufacturing. These sources trace practical development in tubing and powder processing. They do not identify the first PA1012 synthesis or imply that these patent dates mark the polymer’s original invention.', 'pa-multilayer', 'pa-powders');
add(62, 'PA6T was part of mid-twentieth-century research on heat-resistant terephthalamide fibers. Eugene Magat’s DuPont patent, filed in 1952 and granted in 1956, treats straight-chain terephthalamides as known materials whose high melting temperatures hinder ordinary melt spinning. That processing difficulty helped motivate work on modified diamines and copolyamides. Later DuPont research, including Rolando Pagilagan’s 1978 patent, incorporated 6T units into long-chain polyamide compositions for tubing. These records show PA6T chemistry becoming a tool for designing specialized materials. They do not make the commercial introduction dates of modified copolyamides into invention dates for the PA6T homopolymer.', 'magat-6t', 'pagilagan');
add(63, 'Kuraray commercialized its GENESTAR heat-resistant polyamide business in 1999 after developing the material’s distinctive monomer system. PA9T became associated especially with connectors for mobile phones and computers, followed by automotive gears, fuel-system and cooling-system parts. Kuraray’s 2012 report describes expansion beyond the original connector market and continued grade development. Its later product literature distinguishes PA9T from PA9C, including reflector-oriented materials, so not every GENESTAR application should be assigned to PA9T. The 1999 date is a commercial milestone in Kuraray’s PA9T lineage, rather than a date for the entire polyphthalamide family.', 'genestar2012', 'genestar-pa9t');
add(64, 'Unitika records development of XecoT heat-resistant polyamide in 2010. Its subsequent technical announcements identify the base material as PA10T, made using a biomass-derived monomer and developed for demanding molded components. In 2021, Unitika introduced reinforced XecoT grades intended to substitute for PA6T or PA66 during supply shortages, with automotive, electrical and sliding-part applications. This sequence shows PA10T advancing through polymer development and later compounding to meet manufacturing needs. The 2010 milestone is Unitika’s documented product development, not a claim that all PA10T chemistry originated that year.', 'unitika-history', 'xecot2021');
add(65, 'Mitsubishi Gas Chemical began producing MX-Nylon, its MXD6 polyamide, at Niigata in 1984. The company’s historical timeline records expansions in 1991 and 1997 and production in the United States in 2004. Its integrated business linked meta-xylylenediamine manufacture with polymer development. Food-packaging films and PET bottles became important markets because MXD6 could provide a gas-barrier component, while engineering-plastics uses formed another branch. These milestones establish a commercial production history and its main applications; they do not by themselves establish the first laboratory synthesis of the polymer.', 'mgc-mxd6');
add(66, 'Polyphthalamides developed through several semiaromatic polyamide programs, rather than one invention shared by all compositions. A well-documented industrial lineage is Amoco’s Amodel business: development began in the early 1980s, a Greenville semiworks facility followed in 1987, and the first commercial Augusta unit began operating in 1993. Automotive, electronic, consumer and medical-device components became markets. The business passed to Solvay in 2001. Kuraray’s separate 1999 GENESTAR launch illustrates another branch of this family. These histories concern specific commercial compositions and manufacturing programs; they should not be transferred indiscriminately to every PPA or its constituent homopolyamides.', 'amodel-csb', 'genestar2012');
add(67, 'PA6/66 copolyamides developed by combining caprolactam-derived and nylon-66-derived units to adjust behavior for particular applications. A documented packaging milestone is Hartmut Grund’s Naturin patent, filed in 1995 and granted in 1997, which identifies PA6/66 in multilayer tubular films for food casings. BASF’s later work on Ultramid C and multilayer-film recycling shows continued development of this packaging role into the 2020s. These records identify useful application milestones, not the original synthesis date of the copolymer. A PA6/66 chain copolymer also has a different history from a physical blend of separate PA6 and PA66 resins.', 'pa666-film', 'basf-pa-film');

