ref('shugg-history', I, 'Shugg, W. T. Handbook of Electrical and Electronic Insulating Materials, 2nd ed. IEEE Press, 1995. Chapter 1: History, public sample chapter.', 'https://catalogimages.wiley.com/images/db/pdf/0780310306.excerpt.pdf', 'Pages 6–7: mid-1960s DuPont polyimide wire enamels and films; PPS commercial availability in 1972, ICI PESU in 1972–1973 and Amoco Torlon PAI in 1973.');
ref('ultrason', M, 'BASF. Ultrason (PSU, PESU, PPSU): The Specialty Plastic for High-Quality Parts.', 'https://plastics-rubber.basf.com/emea/en/performance_polymers/products/ultrason.html', 'Polymer identities and industrial application overview, including molding, membrane filtration and household uses.');

add(68, 'The modern bisphenol-A polycarbonate industry arose from independent 1953 work by Hermann Schnell at Bayer and Daniel Fox at General Electric. Bayer began industrial Makrolon production in 1958, while GE developed the Lexan business. Tough, transparent articles opened markets extending well beyond the original chemistry programs. GE records NASA helmet applications from 1962 and sheet products from 1968; later automotive lenses, glazing and computer housings became familiar uses. Bayer’s special optical-disc grade supported compact-disc production in 1982. These milestones describe the commercially important BPA-based material, rather than the first preparation of every polymer containing carbonate linkages.', 'covestro-pc', 'ge-lexan50');
add(69, 'Poly(propylene carbonate) belongs to the carbon-dioxide/epoxide copolymerization lineage opened by S. Inoue, H. Koinuma and T. Tsuruta in 1969. Their zinc-based catalyst established a route for incorporating carbon dioxide into an aliphatic polymer, distinct from aromatic engineering polycarbonates. Subsequent researchers improved catalysts, end-group stabilization and blending to address limited conversion, thermal instability and mechanical limitations. By the 2010s and 2020s, published work explored packaging films, biodegradable blends and biomedical materials. These are documented research and application-development directions; the cited review does not establish one universal commercial launch date or show that all proposed uses reached large-scale adoption.', 'ppc-review');
add(70, 'Vinyl fluoride was synthesized by Belgian chemist Frédéric Swarts in 1901; that monomer milestone should be distinguished from the polymer. DuPont dates its PVF development to 1942 and a polymerization patent to 1947. Commercial Tedlar film followed in 1961. Boeing adopted it for aircraft interiors in 1963, and protective architectural and transportation surfaces became enduring applications. Solar-module backsheets formed another important branch: NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory qualified the film in 1986 after a long Department of Energy-sponsored study. Later DuPont developments extended these protective-film applications to flexible and building-integrated photovoltaics.', 'tedlar60');
add(71, 'A foundational patent milestone for polycarbonate–siloxane block copolymers is Howard A. Vaughn Jr.’s General Electric application, filed in 1961 and granted in 1965. It combined polycarbonate and polysiloxane segments and described flexible materials for coatings, electrical insulation and adhesive uses. Later GE research pursued more transparent engineering compositions: a 1996 patent addressed the haze that could accompany siloxane incorporation while retaining the established interest in low-temperature ductility and flame resistance. The history therefore involves controlling block structure and morphology for different applications. The original elastomeric examples and later low-siloxane engineering grades are related developments, rather than one interchangeable formulation.', 'vaughn-pcsiloxane', 'pcsiloxane-haze');
add(72, 'Polyester–polycarbonate development grew alongside early polycarbonate research. In his 1986 oral history, Daniel Fox recalled Eugene Goldberg making polyester carbonates at General Electric during the earlier development period, but their high processing temperatures and viscosity initially limited fabrication to cast films. Fox distinguished that laboratory work from commercial high-heat Lexan products reached much later. Subsequent research continued to tune the ester and carbonate segments: Samyang’s 2018 patent application, claiming 2016 priority, describes block copolymers aimed at optical materials, automotive components and electronic-device housings. These milestones concern chemically joined copolymers; physical polyester/polycarbonate blends have a separate formulation history.', 'fox-oral', 'samyang-pcester');
add(73, 'Polyoxymethylene became a major engineering-plastics family around the beginning of the 1960s through separate homopolymer and copolymer developments. DuPont’s Delrin established the homopolymer branch, while Celanese introduced Celcon in 1962 and Hostaform followed in 1963. The materials found uses in molded mechanical components, including gears, bearings and other precision parts that could replace metal. Subsequent development included stabilized, reinforced and application-specific grades. The family’s history therefore combines improvements in polymer stability with reliable molding and component design; dates associated with one commercial branch should not be applied to all acetal compositions.', 'celanese-pom-guide', 'bpf-acetals');
add(74, 'Formaldehyde polymers were known before the 1920s, but instability limited their usefulness. DuPont research by A. Barkdoll Jr. in 1947 found attractive material from purified formaldehyde, and R. MacDonald led a dedicated development project from 1949–1950. DuPont’s Parkersburg plant brought Delrin into commercial production in 1960. Its practical importance lay in precision mechanical parts, including bearings and other molded components that had often required metal. This development sequence distinguishes early knowledge of formaldehyde polymers from the stabilization, manufacturing and application work needed to establish useful POM homopolymer.', 'hippel-acetals', 'bpf-acetals');
add(75, 'Celanese developed the copolymer branch of commercial POM through an intensive program involving researchers Brown, Barting and Walling. Pilot quantities were produced at Clarkwood, Texas, in April 1961, followed by the Bishop, Texas, production plant in January 1962. Celcon and the 1963 Hostaform introduction established major commercial lineages. Copolymer development offered another approach to producing durable acetal engineering materials for molded mechanisms, gears and precision components. Later grade portfolios expanded through compounding and application-specific stabilization. These milestones distinguish POM copolymers from DuPont’s earlier commercial homopolymer and from simple physical blends of acetal resins.', 'hippel-acetals', 'celanese-pom-guide');
add(76, 'Allan S. Hay discovered oxidative-coupling polymerization of 2,6-dimethylphenol at General Electric in August 1956, obtaining high-molecular-weight poly(phenylene oxide), now commonly called PPE. GE announced the chemistry in 1964, but commercialization also required progress in processing and formulation. Its June 1966 company magazine announced Noryl modified-PPO resins and illustrated pump and valve applications. This established an important distinction: much of PPE’s industrial history belongs to blends designed to process more readily than the neat polymer. Hay’s later account credits a broad GE research effort with developing the chemistry and the commercial resin families.', 'hay-ppe', 'noryl1966');
add(77, 'A. Farnham and R. Johnson obtained high-molecular-weight polysulfone at Union Carbide’s Bound Brook laboratories in 1962. Union Carbide introduced Udel PSU and constructed its Marietta, Ohio, plant in 1965. Applications expanded to electrical connectors, coffee pots, steam trays and microwaveable cookware, followed by plumbing, healthcare and filtration membranes. Amoco acquired the business in 1987 and Solvay in 2001. Later development improved resin purity for membrane manufacture and broadened specialized grades. This history concerns the bisphenol-A polysulfone commercial lineage; PESU and PPSU are chemically distinct members of the wider sulfone-polymer group.', 'udel50');
add(78, 'ICI introduced polyethersulfone in the United Kingdom and then the United States in 1972–1973 under the Victrex name. It joined the emerging range of high-temperature engineering thermoplastics, while retaining a chemical identity distinct from bisphenol-A polysulfone. Later commercial portfolios, including BASF’s Ultrason E, developed applications in heat-resistant molded components and filtration membranes. Household and catering equipment, electrical parts and automotive components became important application areas for this sulfone-resin technology. The ICI dates document commercial introduction; they should not be treated as a single invention date for every aromatic polysulfone.', 'shugg-history', 'ultrason');

