ref('teng-fluoropolymers', 'open_journal', 'Teng, H. Overview of the Development of the Fluoropolymer Industry. Applied Sciences 2012, 2, 496–512.', 'https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/ddcd/cb4c68633bc58bef2e4ac891bce10d26afc3.pdf', 'Pages 500–501: FEP commercialization in 1960, melt-processability and wire insulation, chemical-lining and film applications.');

add(80, 'Phillips Petroleum researchers James T. Edmonds Jr. and Harold W. Hill Jr. documented a practical aromatic-sulfide polymerization route in a patent filed in 1963 and granted in 1967. Phillips brought PPS to market as Ryton in the early 1970s. Its engineering uses expanded through reinforced and other specialized compounds. Toray’s 2006 business account illustrates that later breadth: automotive ignition and headlamp components, copier parts, pumps and valves, as well as insulating films and industrial filtration fibers. PPS history therefore includes the original manufacturing breakthrough and subsequent control of polymer form, compounding and fabrication for particular products.', 'phillips-pps', 'shugg-history', 'toray-pps2006');
add(81, 'Poly(aryl ether ketone)s comprise several related high-performance polymers with distinct development histories. PEKK research associated with the Apollo era preceded the ICI PEEK program whose first documented batch was made in November 1978 and commercialized in 1981. Later industrial development involved companies including Victrex and Arkema, with applications in aerospace composites, demanding mechanical components and medical devices. New product forms included films, fibers, pipes and additive-manufacturing feedstocks. These are family milestones: a PEEK date should not be treated as the invention date for every arrangement of ether and ketone groups within PAEK.', 'victrex40', 'arkema-pekk');
add(82, 'ICI manufactured the first batch in the commercial PEEK lineage on November 19, 1978, and introduced VICTREX PEEK three years later, including glass- and carbon-filled grades. Development subsequently moved beyond resin supply to engineering applications and distinct product forms. Victrex’s 2018 retrospective describes gears, seals, films, pipes and fibers, together with automotive, industrial, electronics and implantable-medical uses. This progression depended on matching a demanding thermoplastic to suitable fabrication and component designs. Later low-melting PAEK composites and other PAEK grades belong to related developments and should not automatically be described as the original PEEK composition.', 'victrex40');
add(83, 'Arkema traces PEKK’s origins to research for the Apollo program in the 1960s. Commercial development required much later work on a difficult and capital-intensive manufacturing process. Following its acquisition of Oxford Performance Materials in 2009, Arkema developed the Kepstan business and collaborated with PCAS to establish industrial production in France. Aerospace and offshore oil-and-gas components became important target markets, while carbon-fiber composites and additive manufacturing broadened the application range. This history separates early polymer research from later scale-up and illustrates why PEKK’s commercial trajectory differs from that of PEEK, despite their shared PAEK family.', 'arkema-pekk');
add(84, 'General Electric’s Ultem polyetherimide entered the market in 1982. Heat-resistant headlamp reflectors were an early automotive application, and later development expanded the material into electrical and electronic components. The business subsequently continued under SABIC, whose portfolio documents reinforced PEI for connectors and structural brackets, as well as specialized blends for medical devices, aerospace interiors and additive manufacturing. This development history reflects both the base polyetherimide and later grade design. The Ultem launch identifies a major commercial milestone, while individual reinforced, blended and siloxane-containing products have their own later development histories.', 'ultem1982', 'ultem-portfolio');
add(85, 'Polyamide-imides developed as heat-resistant materials combining amide and imide chemistry. Amoco brought Torlon to market in 1973, establishing an important engineering-resin lineage. Related Standard Oil research by Robert B. Hanson and James R. Stephens, filed in 1973 and patented in 1974, developed film-forming PAI for electrical wire enamels, insulating coatings and shaped articles. Their work illustrates the importance of controlling precursor formation and subsequent conversion during fabrication. These documented commercialization and application milestones do not establish the first synthesis of every PAI composition; wire-coating systems and molded engineering grades should retain their own formulation histories.', 'shugg-history', 'hanson-pai');
add(86, 'Polyimide chemistry has early-twentieth-century antecedents, but useful aromatic engineering polyimides emerged from a much later development period. William M. Edwards and I. M. Robinson’s DuPont patent, granted in 1955, documents pyromellitic-acid polyimides and their preparation. DuPont’s subsequent commercial aromatic-polyimide development helped establish heat-resistant films and electrical-insulation applications. Aerospace, microelectronics, membranes and composite matrices became major fields of use. Research later diversified substantially: work since 2010 on porous and crosslinked polyimides explored gas separation, catalysis and energy storage. The broad PI class has multiple monomer systems and architectures, so it has no single commercial introduction applicable to every member.', 'edwards-pi', 'pi-porous-review', 'shugg-history');
add(87, 'Thermoplastic polyimides developed through efforts to make heat-resistant imide polymers suitable for melt fabrication. Mitsui Toatsu’s AURUM lineage is documented by a Canadian trademark application filed in 1989 and a declaration of use in 1994. Those are commercial-record milestones, not a claimed first synthesis of the entire class. Mitsui Chemicals’ subsequent technical literature describes injection-molded and extruded materials for precision machinery, semiconductor equipment, electrical connectors, wire coatings and transportation components. Reinforcement and wear-resistant formulations extended this application range. Different thermoplastic PI chemistries have separate histories, and AURUM’s chronology should not be generalized to every melt-processable polyimide.', 'aurum-cipo', 'aurum');
add(89, 'Roy Plunkett and his assistant Jack Rebok discovered PTFE on April 6, 1938, while investigating tetrafluoroethylene refrigerant chemistry in the DuPont-associated Kinetic Chemicals program. Turning the unexpected solid into usable products required further engineering because it was difficult to shape. Its early wartime application was corrosion-resistant valves and seals for uranium-hexafluoride handling in the Manhattan Project. The Teflon trademark was registered in 1945. Nonstick cookware emerged during the 1950s and reached the U.S. market in 1961 through Marion A. Trozzolo. PTFE’s history thus progressed from an accidental laboratory finding to specialized industrial service and later familiar household coatings.', 'aps-ptfe');
add(90, 'DuPont commercially introduced FEP in 1960 as part of the effort to retain useful fluoropolymer characteristics while making fabrication easier than with PTFE. Copolymerizing tetrafluoroethylene with hexafluoropropylene created a melt-processable material suitable for extrusion, molding and films. Electrical-wire insulation, including plenum and network cables, became a major market; chemical-resistant pipe and tank linings, nonstick surfaces and solar-related films provided other applications. By 2012, the industrial literature documented commercial products from DuPont, Daikin and Dyneon. The 1960 milestone is FEP’s market introduction, distinct from Plunkett’s earlier discovery of the PTFE homopolymer.', 'teng-fluoropolymers');


ref('ppsu-johnson', P, 'Johnson, R. N.; Farnham, A. G. Polyarylene Polyethers. US 4,108,837, 1978, assigned to Union Carbide.', 'https://patents.google.com/patent/US4108837A/en', 'Patent family beginning in 1963; description of polyarylene-polyether preparation. Identified as a PPSU preparation antecedent in Savariar’s 2000 patent.');
ref('ppsu-savariar', P, 'Savariar, S. Poly(biphenyl Ether Sulfone). WO 2000/018824, 2000, assigned to BP Amoco.', 'https://patents.google.com/patent/WO2000018824A1/en', 'Background and invention: Radel R identity, Johnson/Farnham antecedents, lower-oligomer and melt-flow development; medical trays, cookware, aircraft and plumbing parts.');
ref('radel35', M, 'Syensqo. Syensqo Celebrates 35 Years of Radel PPSU Innovations with Launch of White Radel. April 29, 2025.', 'https://www.syensqo.com/sites/g/files/alwlxe161/files/2025-04/Syensqo-celebrates-35-years-of-Radel-PPSU-innovations-with-launch-of-White-Radel.pdf', 'Pages 1–2: 35-year product anniversary, healthcare applications, white medical-grade development and additive-manufacturing forms.', 'syensqo');
ref('radel-markets', M, 'Syensqo. Radel PPSU Polyphenylsulfone.', 'https://www.syensqo.com/en/brands/radel-ppsu', 'Market Applications: sterilizable trays and instrument handles, plumbing, food service, aircraft and wire insulation.', 'syensqo');
add(79, 'PPSU grew from aromatic polyether-sulfone chemistry developed at Union Carbide by Robert N. Johnson and Alford G. Farnham; their patent lineage began in 1963 and a 1978 patent was later cited as a preparation route for the biphenyl-containing polymer. This establishes a research antecedent, not a definitive first-invention date. Commercial development continued through Amoco’s Radel R business. Selvaraj Savariar’s 2000 BP Amoco patent addressed low-molecular-weight material and melt flow, identifying medical trays, cookware, aircraft and plumbing parts as applications. Sterilizable surgical-instrument handles and trays became prominent markets, alongside hot-water fittings and food-service components. In 2025, Syensqo marked 35 years of its Radel PPSU product lineage and introduced a white medical grade; that anniversary should not be mistaken for the beginning of the underlying chemistry. Later product development also extended PPSU into filaments for additive manufacturing.', 'ppsu-johnson', 'ppsu-savariar', 'radel35', 'radel-markets');

ref('pk-xiao2024', 'open_journal', 'Xiao, X.; Zheng, H.; Gao, H.; Cheng, Z.; Feng, C.; Yang, J.; Gao, H. Recent Advances in Synthesis of Non-Alternating Polyketone Generated by Copolymerization of Carbon Monoxide and Ethylene. International Journal of Molecular Sciences 2024, 25, 1348.', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10816092/', 'Introduction: 1951 metal-catalyzed precursor chemistry, Drent’s 1991 catalyst report, Shell Carilon in 1996 and discontinuation in 2000, Hyosung in 2013; distinction from later non-alternating polymers.');
